  "grafo_evidencia": {
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d0359363-7ca5-5377-89da-7e3b46ac8767",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John Charles Cutler\n- Organizations: United States Public Health Service\n- Events: Tuskegee Study of Untreated Syphilis in the Negro Male\n- Places: Alabama\n- Concepts: syphilis, untreated syphilis, venereal disease program\n\nFacts:\n- John Charles Cutler was born on June 29, 1915, and he later became a senior surgeon and the acting chief of the venereal disease program in the United States Public Health Service.\n- The Tuskegee Study of Untreated Syphilis in the Negro Male, also known as the Tuskegee Syphilis Study or Tuskegee Syphilis Experiment, was conducted by the United States Public Health Service from 1932 to 1972.\n- The Tuskegee Syphilis Experiment was intended to observe the natural progression of untreated syphilis in rural African-American men in Alabama while presenting itself as free health care from the United States government.\n- John Charles Cutler died on February 8, 2003.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "c1458a7c-ecb8-5736-b0d1-bc17ccab0079",
        "text": "Page: John Charles Cutler. Sentence: John Charles Cutler (June 29, 1915 – February 8, 2003) was a senior surgeon, and the acting chief of the venereal disease program in the United States Public Health Service.\nPage: Tuskegee syphilis experiment. Sentence: The Tuskegee Study of Untreated Syphilis in the Negro Male, also known as the Tuskegee Syphilis Study or Tuskegee Syphilis Experiment ( ) was an infamous clinical study conducted between 1932 and 1972 by the U.S. Public Health Service.\nPage: Tuskegee syphilis experiment. Sentence: The purpose of this study was to observe the natural progression of untreated syphilis in rural African-American men in Alabama under the guise of receiving free health care from the United States government.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
